---
import Slide from '@decks/templates/Slide.astro';

// The spine of the Anvil segment, stated rather than implied. Neither
// source deck had this slide, and without it the five that follow read
// as a tour of artifacts instead of as evidence for a position.
//
// It is built to be WALKED, and it took three passes to get there.
// One column of eight at 48px was the first attempt and it was a wall:
// eight lines of heavy type under eight bright accent ticks, which is a
// lot of shouting for a list nobody is meant to read ahead on. Shrinking
// it fixed the weight and broke the composition, leaving a small block
// floating in the top left of a 1920 slide.
//
// Two columns of four at 36px is the answer. Light enough to be a list
// rather than a headline, wide enough to look composed, and short enough
// that it does not need the whole slide.
//
// `grid-flow-col` with `grid-rows-4` is load-bearing. Default row-major
// flow would read 1,2 across then 3,4 across, which scrambles both the
// walk order and the grouping. Filling column-major keeps the reading
// order top-to-bottom-then-over, and puts the four architecture
// decisions in the left column and the four about keeping it alive in
// the right. Nothing labels those groups; the split just rewards anyone
// who notices.
//
// The order is not arbitrary. The left column gets paid off by slides 12
// to 15. The right column is how it stayed alive, and only Adoption gets
// its own slide; the rest are said here and here only.
//
// "Nothing in the system is permanent" is the one to linger on, because
// it is the one that pays off twice: it is why multiple frontend
// libraries were allowed to coexist, and it is why the Sketch to Figma
// pivot cost a pipeline instead of the system. Slide 17 comes back to it.
//
// The checkmark next to a list of decisions, in the deck whose next
// segment is a Checklist component, is a coincidence. It is not worth
// pointing out and it is not why the marks are there.
//
// Sizes are arbitrary pixel values on purpose. This deck overrides
// Tailwind's spacing scale (--spacing-1..12 is 8/16/24/32/48/64/...),
// so `size-9` is 120px here and `gap-6` is 64px, not the 36 and 24 you
// would get from stock Tailwind. The first draft used those and
// overflowed the slide by a long way. Anything that has to fit exactly
// gets a bracketed value.

const decisions = [
	'Code is the source of truth',
	'Multiple frontend libraries can coexist',
	'Nothing in the system is permanent',
	'Design assets stay in sync with code',
	'Documentation is a first-class citizen',
	'Adoption is earned, not mandated',
	'Accessibility is baked in, not bolted on',
	'Teams contribute, and own what is not yet systemized',
];
---

<Slide title="Anvil Design System" subtitle="Key Decisions">
	<div
		class="grid grid-flow-col grid-rows-4 justify-start gap-x-[96px] gap-y-[40px]"
	>
		{
			decisions.map((d) => (
				<div class="flex items-center gap-[22px]">
					<svg
						class="text-accent block h-[30px] w-[30px] flex-none"
						viewBox="0 0 24 24"
						fill="none"
						stroke="currentColor"
						stroke-width="2.5"
						stroke-linecap="round"
						stroke-linejoin="round"
						aria-hidden="true"
					>
						<path d="M20 6 9 17l-5-5" />
					</svg>
					<span class="text-strong text-4xl">{d}</span>
				</div>
			))
		}
	</div>
</Slide>
